window.MOLASTRIA_NEQUIP = {"index":{"title":"NequIP","sub":"A neural-network potential that turns with the molecule","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · EQUIVARIANT INTERATOMIC POTENTIALS","h1":"NequIP","question":"How can a model trained on 133 structures be more accurate than one trained on 133 500?","lead":"NequIP (Neural Equivariant Interatomic Potentials) is a machine-learned interatomic potential proposed in 2021 by Kozinsky’s group at Harvard and published in Nature Communications in 2022. It treats molecules and materials as neighbour graphs whose features are not just numbers but vectors and higher-order tensors that rotate exactly with the structure. The result is an accurate force field trained on very little quantum-chemistry data. This section has eight chapters: why machine-learned potentials are needed, symmetry, spherical harmonics and irreducible representations, tensor products, the NequIP architecture, forces and energy conservation, the paper’s experiments, and how training and deployment work today, with Allegro, MACE and foundation models. All ten demos are computed live in the browser.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (derived rigorously from group theory or calculus), established by benchmarks (supported by tests in the paper and later work), approximation, frontier (still changing fast) and open. Demos carry one of three labels: exact (computed live from the definition), toy model (built to show one thing clearly, not NequIP itself) and paper data (taken as they are from the paper’s tables). Numbers from the paper come from version 3 on arXiv; software usage follows the official documentation, which takes precedence after new releases. No NequIP code is copied here: the maths of the demos is written from the formulas of the paper and textbooks.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Derived rigorously from group theory, linear algebra or calculus"],"tested":["Established by benchmarks","Supported by tests in the paper and in later independent work, and in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Developing fast; the best models and numbers are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from the definition or a closed formula (assumptions are listed under each demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; not NequIP itself"],"data":["Paper data","Numbers taken as they are from the paper’s tables; the demo only plots and fits them"]},"groups":[{"title":"The problem and its symmetry","ids":["why","symmetry"]},{"title":"The mathematics of equivariance","ids":["irreps","tensor"]},{"title":"The model","ids":["arch","forces"]},{"title":"Results and practice","ids":["results","practice"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"why","n":"01","title":"Why machine-learned interatomic potentials","summary":"Molecular dynamics needs the forces at every step; quantum chemistry gets them right but is too slow, classical force fields are fast but their functional form is fixed in advance. Machine-learned potentials want both: learn the potential-energy surface from quantum-chemistry data, then run at close to force-field speed. The road from Behler–Parrinello, GAP and SchNet to NequIP, and the one assumption all of them share: the energy can be split into local contributions from each atom.","level":"history","demos":["pes"]},{"id":"symmetry","n":"02","title":"Symmetry: invariance and equivariance","summary":"E(3) is the group of translations, rotations and inversions of three-dimensional space. The energy does not change under them, while forces and dipoles rotate along: that is “equivariance”. Why using only invariants (distances, angles) as features loses information, what equivariant networks gain by keeping directions in the features, and permutation symmetry and parity.","level":"proved","demos":["equiv"]},{"id":"irreps","n":"03","title":"Spherical harmonics and irreducible representations","summary":"The smallest building blocks under rotation that cannot be split further are the irreducible representations of order l, with 2l + 1 components; l = 0 is a scalar, l = 1 a vector, l = 2 behaves like a symmetric traceless matrix. Spherical harmonics are their most natural basis, and Wigner D matrices describe how they mix under rotation. NequIP’s features written as “32x0e + 32x1o + …” use exactly this language.","level":"proved","demos":["sh"]},{"id":"tensor","n":"04","title":"Tensor products and Clebsch–Gordan coefficients","summary":"The product of two irreducible representations splits back into irreducible representations: l₁ ⊗ l₂ = |l₁ − l₂| ⊕ … ⊕ (l₁ + l₂), with Clebsch–Gordan coefficients. The tensor product of two vectors gives the dot product (l = 0), the cross product (l = 1) and a symmetric traceless tensor (l = 2). NequIP’s convolution is “neighbour feature ⊗ spherical harmonics of the neighbour direction”, with a distance-dependent weight on every allowed path.","level":"proved","demos":["tp"]},{"id":"arch","n":"05","title":"The NequIP architecture: from atomic species to one energy","summary":"NequIP treats a structure as a neighbour graph: every atom is a node, joined to the atoms within r_c. Atomic species are first embedded as scalar features, then pass through several interaction blocks (equivariant convolution, self-interaction, gated nonlinearity, residual update), and finally each atom outputs one scalar energy whose sum is the total. Distances are expanded in 8 Bessel functions with an envelope and fed to a small multilayer perceptron. The number of layers × r_c sets the receptive field.","level":"tested","demos":["radial","graph"]},{"id":"forces","n":"06","title":"Energy, forces and stress: why forces come from the energy","summary":"NequIP predicts only a scalar energy; forces are its negative gradient F = −∇E, obtained exactly by automatic differentiation, and stress is the derivative of the energy with respect to strain. Such forces are automatically equivariant and conservative: the work around any closed path is zero and the total energy does not drift in NVE simulations. Models that predict forces directly can be faster, but their force field has a little curl, which causes trouble in long simulations.","level":"proved","demos":["nve"]},{"id":"results","n":"07","title":"The paper’s experiments: accuracy, data efficiency and learning curves","summary":"On the ten small molecules of revised MD-17, with the same 1000 configurations, going from l = 0 to l ≥ 1 cuts force errors to between a half and a twentieth of their former size (depending on the molecule); on water and ice, NequIP trained on 133 structures has lower force errors than DeepMD trained on 133 500. The LiPS learning curve is a straight line on log–log axes, and the equivariant model’s slope is steeper than that of a scalar-only one. Why this is so, the paper says, still lacks a clear explanation.","level":"tested","demos":["curve","gpfit"]},{"id":"practice","n":"08","title":"Hands-on and beyond: training, deployment, Allegro, MACE and foundation models","summary":"Today’s nequip is a framework: nequip-train trains from a YAML configuration, nequip-package packages models for distribution, nequip-compile compiles them for ASE or LAMMPS, and the GPU kernels can be swapped for accelerated ones such as OpenEquivariance. The same framework includes Allegro, strictly local and scalable to hundreds of millions of atoms. MACE trades a higher many-body order for fewer layers. In 2026 NequIP and Allegro also gained pretrained foundation models covering most of the periodic table. All of them are trustworthy only within the chemical space their training data cover.","level":"frontier","demos":["cost"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"2007","text":"Behler and Parrinello: one neural network per atom, total energy as a sum of atomic energies (<a href=\"#c-why\" data-open=\"why\">01 · Why machine-learned interatomic potentials</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-behler2007\" data-ref=\"behler2007\">Behler &amp; Parrinello 2007</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2010","text":"Gaussian approximation potentials, GAP <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bartok2010\" data-ref=\"bartok2010\">Bartók et al. 2010</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2016","text":"Group-equivariant convolutional networks: symmetry written into the network’s structure <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cohen2016\" data-ref=\"cohen2016\">Cohen &amp; Welling 2016</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2017","text":"SchNet <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schutt2017\" data-ref=\"schutt2017\">Schütt et al. 2017</a>]</span>, message-passing networks <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gilmer2017\" data-ref=\"gilmer2017\">Gilmer et al. 2017</a>]</span> and ANI <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-smith2017\" data-ref=\"smith2017\">Smith et al. 2017</a>]</span>; GDML and the MD-17 dataset <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2018","text":"Tensor field networks bring spherical harmonics and tensor products to 3D point clouds (<a href=\"#c-tensor\" data-open=\"tensor\">04 · Tensor products and Clebsch–Gordan coefficients</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a>]</span>; DeepMD <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhang2018\" data-ref=\"zhang2018\">Zhang et al. 2018</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2019","text":"The atomic cluster expansion, ACE <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-drautz2019\" data-ref=\"drautz2019\">Drautz 2019</a>]</span>; Cormorant <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anderson2019\" data-ref=\"anderson2019\">Anderson et al. 2019</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2020","text":"DimeNet’s Bessel basis and smooth envelope (<a href=\"#c-arch\" data-open=\"arch\">05 · The NequIP architecture: from atomic species to one energy</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a>]</span>; revised MD-17 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a>]</span>; active learning with FLARE <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-vandermause2020\" data-ref=\"vandermause2020\">Vandermause et al. 2020</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2021","text":"The NequIP preprint (January); PaiNN <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schutt2021\" data-ref=\"schutt2021\">Schütt et al. 2021</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2022","text":"NequIP published in Nature Communications (<a href=\"#c-results\" data-open=\"results\">07 · The paper’s experiments: accuracy, data efficiency and learning curves</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>; the e3nn paper <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>]</span>; MACE <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2023","text":"Allegro published (<a href=\"#c-practice\" data-open=\"practice\">08 · Hands-on and beyond: training, deployment, Allegro, MACE and foundation models</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>]</span> and large-scale biomolecular simulations reported at SC23 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023sc\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023sc\">Musaelian et al. 2023b</a>]</span>; the SPICE dataset <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eastman2023\" data-ref=\"eastman2023\">Eastman et al. 2023</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2024","text":"The OMat24 dataset <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-barroso2024\" data-ref=\"barroso2024\">Barroso-Luque et al. 2024</a>]</span>; a systematic study of non-conservative forces (<a href=\"#c-forces\" data-open=\"forces\">06 · Energy, forces and stress: why forces come from the energy</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bigi2024\" data-ref=\"bigi2024\">Bigi et al. 2024</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025","text":"nequip 0.7.0 (23 April): a rewrite of the framework into train, package and compile steps. A review of the design space of equivariant atomic potentials <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2025\" data-ref=\"batatia2025\">Batatia et al. 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026","text":"Faster training and inference <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tan2026\" data-ref=\"tan2026\">Tan et al. 2026</a>]</span>; NequIP and Allegro foundation models <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kavanagh2026\" data-ref=\"kavanagh2026\">Kavanagh et al. 2026</a>]</span>; nequip 0.19.2 (16 November) is the latest release at the time of writing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A model predicts the force on every atom. If the whole molecule is rotated by 90°, what should an equivariant model’s forces do?","opts":["Stay exactly the same","Rotate by 90° with unchanged magnitudes","Change magnitude but not direction","Depend on the training data"],"a":1,"why":"Energy is invariant and force is a vector, which must be equivariant: F(Rx) = R F(x). In the equivariance demo the solid and dashed arrows coincide exactly.","ch":"symmetry"},{"q":"How many components does the irreducible representation with l = 2 have?","opts":["2","3","5","9"],"a":2,"why":"2l + 1 = 5, the same as the number of d orbitals. 9 is the component count of an ordinary 3 × 3 matrix, which splits into 1 + 3 + 5.","ch":"irreps"},{"q":"Which irreducible representations does the tensor product of two ordinary vectors (1o) produce?","opts":["0e, 1o, 2e","0e, 1e, 2e","Only 0e","0o, 1o, 2o"],"a":1,"why":"l runs from |1 − 1| to 1 + 1, and the parity is (−1)(−1) = +1. So the dot product is a scalar (0e) and the cross product a pseudovector (1e).","ch":"tensor"},{"q":"Which part of NequIP’s convolution is learned?","opts":["The spherical harmonics","The Clebsch–Gordan coefficients","The radial function, which depends only on distance","The rotation matrices"],"a":2,"why":"The directional parts (spherical harmonics, CG coefficients) are fixed mathematical objects; the learned radial function sees only the scalar distance, so equivariance comes from construction, not training.","ch":"tensor"},{"q":"Why is the radial function multiplied by an envelope that goes smoothly to zero at r_c?","opts":["To reduce the number of parameters","So energy and forces stay continuous as atoms cross the cutoff sphere; otherwise MD gets kicks","To make the model equivariant","To speed up training"],"a":1,"why":"The Bessel functions vanish at r_c but their slopes do not, so the forces jump. The DimeNet polynomial envelope makes the value and first two derivatives vanish.","ch":"arch"},{"q":"For a NequIP model with 6 layers and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å, how far away can the atoms that an atomic energy depends on be?","opts":["5 Å","10 Å","30 Å","There is no limit"],"a":2,"why":"Each layer passes information one step along the graph, so 6 layers reach at most 6 × 5 = 30 Å. Allegro stays at 5 Å regardless of the number of layers.","ch":"arch"},{"q":"Why are NequIP’s forces automatically conservative?","opts":["Because training penalises energy drift","Because the forces are the negative gradient of one scalar energy, so their curl is zero","Because the network is equivariant","Because Verlet integration is used"],"a":1,"why":"A gradient field has zero curl and does no work around a closed path. Equivariance makes forces rotate correctly but does not make them conservative; equivariant direct-force models can also drift.","ch":"forces"},{"q":"How many training labels does one DFT calculation on a 20-atom structure provide (energy and forces only)?","opts":["1","20","60","61"],"a":3,"why":"1 energy plus 3 × 20 = 60 force components: 61 in all.","ch":"forces"},{"q":"What did the paper find happens to the learning curve (error against training-set size, log–log) when only l is changed from 0 to 1 or more?","opts":["The whole line shifts with unchanged slope","The slope becomes steeper","It is no longer straight","Nothing changes"],"a":1,"why":"At l = 0 the slope resembles other methods, while l ≥ 1 is steeper. Why this happens, the paper says, still lacks a clear explanation.","ch":"results"},{"q":"Why is Allegro better suited than NequIP to simulating a large system split over many GPUs?","opts":["Allegro has a higher l_max","Allegro is strictly local, so each piece exchanges only a boundary one r_c thick","Allegro does not need forces","Allegro is not equivariant"],"a":1,"why":"NequIP’s receptive field is layers × r_c, so the boundary thickens with depth; Allegro’s information never leaves r_c. Both are equivariant.","ch":"practice"},{"q":"A pretrained foundation model gives low force errors on your system. What does that show?","opts":["The simulation will be stable and the properties correct","The training data cover these configurations, but stability and properties still need checking","No further comparison with DFT is needed","It can be used for excited states"],"a":1,"why":"Low force error ≠ correct simulation (Fu et al. 2023). Long-run stability and the physical quantities you care about still need checking.","ch":"practice"}],"books":[{"title":"Machine Learning Meets Quantum Physics","authors":"K. T. Schütt, S. Chmiela, O. A. von Lilienfeld, A. Tkatchenko, K. Tsuda, K.-R. Müller (eds.) (Springer, Lecture Notes in Physics 968, 2020)","note":"A collection on machine-learned potentials and molecular property prediction, from kernel methods to neural networks."},{"title":"Understanding Molecular Simulation","authors":"Daan Frenkel, Berend Smit (Academic Press, 3rd ed. 2023)","note":"The standard textbook of molecular dynamics and Monte Carlo: integrators, ensembles and how to get physical quantities from trajectories."},{"title":"Group Theory: Application to the Physics of Condensed Matter","authors":"M. S. Dresselhaus, G. Dresselhaus, A. Jorio (Springer, 2008)","note":"Representations, irreducible representations, direct products and selection rules from a physicist’s angle: background for chapters 3 and 4."},{"title":"Angular Momentum in Quantum Mechanics","authors":"A. R. Edmonds (Princeton University Press, 1957)","note":"The classic reference on spherical harmonics, Wigner D matrices and Clebsch–Gordan coefficients."},{"title":"Geometric Deep Learning: Grids, Groups, Graphs, Geodesics, and Gauges","authors":"M. M. Bronstein, J. Bruna, T. Cohen, P. Veličković (arXiv:2104.13478, 2021)","note":"A long text placing convolutional, graph and equivariant networks in one framework of symmetry."},{"title":"NequIP documentation","authors":"mir-group (nequip.readthedocs.io, MIT licence)","note":"The official documentation: installation, configuration, training, packaging, compilation, ASE and LAMMPS interfaces and foundation models. It is authoritative for the commands in chapter 8."}],"open":[{"q":"Why does equivariance steepen the learning curve rather than merely lowering the error overall?","ch":"results"},{"q":"How much does the many-body information from message passing actually contribute? Is a higher body order or more layers the better buy?","ch":"arch"},{"q":"How can long-range electrostatics, charge transfer and different spin states be built into local equivariant potentials without losing linear cost?","ch":"practice"},{"q":"Can direct-force models be both fast and stable in long simulations?","ch":"forces"},{"q":"Can pretrained foundation models reliably tell users “I have not seen this configuration”?","ch":"practice"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have opened are ticked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years (preprints by the year of the preprint).","path":"To read on: <a href=\"../ai-math/#t-39\">Groups, equivariance and molecular models</a> in Frontier AI Maths covers the same symmetry from the mathematical side; the <a href=\"../chemistry/computational/#c-md\">molecular dynamics</a> chapter of the Computational Chemistry hall covers the step NequIP replaces; <a href=\"../macro/\">Heat, Statistics and Flow</a> explains how simulated trajectories become temperatures, entropies and diffusion coefficients; the <a href=\"../open-models/\">Open Model Hall</a> collects comparable pretrained models such as Meta’s UMA, and <a href=\"../models/\">the model interface</a> runs some of them online; and the <a href=\"../code/\">Code Desk</a> writes inputs and reads outputs for programs such as xTB, ORCA and PySCF, useful when preparing training data.","library":"36 references. Those with a DOI were looked up in OpenAlex (which draws on Crossref) for title, authors, year and volume; those with only an arXiv number were checked on arXiv (November 2026; node tools/nequip-refs.mjs re-checks them)."}},"refs":[{"id":"behler2007","authors":"J. Behler, M. Parrinello","year":2007,"title":"Generalized neural-network representation of high-dimensional potential-energy surfaces","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"98","pages":"146401","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.98.146401","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why"]},{"id":"bartok2010","authors":"A. P. Bartók, M. C. Payne, R. Kondor, G. Csányi","year":2010,"title":"Gaussian approximation potentials: the accuracy of quantum mechanics, without the electrons","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"104","pages":"136403","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.104.136403","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","results"]},{"id":"cohen2016","authors":"T. S. Cohen, M. Welling","year":2016,"title":"Group equivariant convolutional networks","journal":"Proceedings of the 33rd International Conference on Machine Learning","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1602.07576","url":"","note":"","used":["symmetry"]},{"id":"chmiela2017","authors":"S. Chmiela, A. Tkatchenko, H. E. Sauceda, I. Poltavsky, K. T. Schütt, K.-R. Müller","year":2017,"title":"Machine learning of accurate energy-conserving molecular force fields","journal":"Science Advances","vol":"3","pages":"e1603015","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.1603015","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","forces","results"]},{"id":"gilmer2017","authors":"J. Gilmer, S. S. Schoenholz, P. F. Riley, O. Vinyals, G. E. Dahl","year":2017,"title":"Neural message passing for quantum chemistry","journal":"Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Machine Learning","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1704.01212","url":"","note":"","used":["why"]},{"id":"hestness2017","authors":"J. Hestness, S. Narang, N. Ardalani, G. Diamos, H. Jun, H. Kianinejad, M. M. A. Patwary, Y. Yang, Y. Zhou","year":2017,"title":"Deep learning scaling is predictable, empirically","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1712.00409","url":"","note":"","used":["results"]},{"id":"larsen2017","authors":"A. H. Larsen et al.","year":2017,"title":"The atomic simulation environment—a Python library for working with atoms","journal":"Journal of Physics: Condensed Matter","vol":"29","pages":"273002","doi":"10.1088/1361-648X/aa680e","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"schutt2017","authors":"K. T. Schütt, P.-J. Kindermans, H. E. Sauceda, S. Chmiela, A. Tkatchenko, K.-R. Müller","year":2017,"title":"SchNet: A continuous-filter convolutional neural network for modeling quantum interactions","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 30","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1706.08566","url":"","note":"","used":["why","arch"]},{"id":"smith2017","authors":"J. S. Smith, O. Isayev, A. E. Roitberg","year":2017,"title":"ANI-1: an extensible neural network potential with DFT accuracy at force field computational cost","journal":"Chemical Science","vol":"8","pages":"3192–3203","doi":"10.1039/C6SC05720A","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","results"]},{"id":"chmiela2018","authors":"S. Chmiela, H. E. Sauceda, K.-R. Müller, A. Tkatchenko","year":2018,"title":"Towards exact molecular dynamics simulations with machine-learned force fields","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"9","pages":"3887","doi":"10.1038/s41467-018-06169-2","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","results"]},{"id":"thomas2018","authors":"N. Thomas, T. Smidt, S. Kearnes, L. Yang, L. Li, K. Kohlhoff, P. Riley","year":2018,"title":"Tensor field networks: Rotation- and translation-equivariant neural networks for 3D point clouds","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1802.08219","url":"","note":"","used":["symmetry","tensor"]},{"id":"weiler2018","authors":"M. Weiler, M. Geiger, M. Welling, W. Boomsma, T. Cohen","year":2018,"title":"3D steerable CNNs: Learning rotationally equivariant features in volumetric data","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 31","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1807.02547","url":"","note":"","used":["symmetry","arch"]},{"id":"zhang2018","authors":"L. Zhang, J. Han, H. Wang, R. Car, W. E","year":2018,"title":"Deep potential molecular dynamics: a scalable model with the accuracy of quantum mechanics","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"120","pages":"143001","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.120.143001","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","results"]},{"id":"anderson2019","authors":"B. Anderson, T.-S. Hy, R. Kondor","year":2019,"title":"Cormorant: Covariant molecular neural networks","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 32","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1906.04015","url":"","note":"","used":["arch"]},{"id":"cheng2019","authors":"B. Cheng, E. A. Engel, J. Behler, C. Dellago, M. Ceriotti","year":2019,"title":"Ab initio thermodynamics of liquid and solid water","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","vol":"116","pages":"1110–1115","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1815117116","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["results"]},{"id":"drautz2019","authors":"R. Drautz","year":2019,"title":"Atomic cluster expansion for accurate and transferable interatomic potentials","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"99","pages":"014104","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.99.014104","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","practice"]},{"id":"christensen2020","authors":"A. S. Christensen, O. A. von Lilienfeld","year":2020,"title":"On the role of gradients for machine learning of molecular energies and forces","journal":"Machine Learning: Science and Technology","vol":"1","pages":"045018","doi":"10.1088/2632-2153/abba6f","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["forces","results"]},{"id":"klicpera2020","authors":"J. Klicpera (Gasteiger), J. Groß, S. Günnemann","year":2020,"title":"Directional message passing for molecular graphs","journal":"International Conference on Learning Representations","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2003.03123","url":"","note":"","used":["why","arch"]},{"id":"vandermause2020","authors":"J. Vandermause, S. B. Torrisi, S. Batzner, Y. Xie, L. Sun, A. M. Kolpak, B. Kozinsky","year":2020,"title":"On-the-fly active learning of interpretable Bayesian force fields for atomistic rare events","journal":"npj Computational Materials","vol":"6","pages":"20","doi":"10.1038/s41524-020-0283-z","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"kovacs2021","authors":"D. P. Kovács, C. van der Oord, J. Kucera, A. E. A. Allen, D. J. Cole, C. Ortner, G. Csányi","year":2021,"title":"Linear atomic cluster expansion force fields for organic molecules: beyond RMSE","journal":"Journal of Chemical Theory and Computation","vol":"17","pages":"7696–7711","doi":"10.1021/acs.jctc.1c00647","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["results"]},{"id":"park2021","authors":"C. W. Park, M. Kornbluth, J. Vandermause, C. Wolverton, B. Kozinsky, J. P. Mailoa","year":2021,"title":"Accurate and scalable graph neural network force field and molecular dynamics with direct force architecture","journal":"npj Computational Materials","vol":"7","pages":"73","doi":"10.1038/s41524-021-00543-3","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["arch"]},{"id":"schutt2021","authors":"K. T. Schütt, O. T. Unke, M. Gastegger","year":2021,"title":"Equivariant message passing for the prediction of tensorial properties and molecular spectra","journal":"Proceedings of the 38th International Conference on Machine Learning","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2102.03150","url":"","note":"","used":["arch"]},{"id":"unke2021","authors":"O. T. Unke, S. Chmiela, H. E. Sauceda, M. Gastegger, I. Poltavsky, K. T. Schütt, A. Tkatchenko, K.-R. Müller","year":2021,"title":"Machine learning force fields","journal":"Chemical Reviews","vol":"121","pages":"10142–10186","doi":"10.1021/acs.chemrev.0c01111","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why"]},{"id":"batatia2022","authors":"I. Batatia, D. P. Kovács, G. N. C. Simm, C. Ortner, G. Csányi","year":2022,"title":"MACE: Higher order equivariant message passing neural networks for fast and accurate force fields","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2206.07697","url":"","note":"","used":["tensor","practice"]},{"id":"batzner2022","authors":"S. Batzner, A. Musaelian, L. Sun, M. Geiger, J. P. Mailoa, M. Kornbluth, N. Molinari, T. E. Smidt, B. Kozinsky","year":2022,"title":"E(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"13","pages":"2453","doi":"10.1038/s41467-022-29939-5","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["why","symmetry","irreps","tensor","arch","forces","results","practice"]},{"id":"geiger2022","authors":"M. Geiger, T. Smidt","year":2022,"title":"e3nn: Euclidean neural networks","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2207.09453","url":"","note":"","used":["symmetry","irreps"]},{"id":"thompson2022","authors":"A. P. Thompson et al.","year":2022,"title":"LAMMPS - a flexible simulation tool for particle-based materials modeling at the atomic, meso, and continuum scales","journal":"Computer Physics Communications","vol":"271","pages":"108171","doi":"10.1016/j.cpc.2021.108171","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"eastman2023","authors":"P. Eastman, P. K. Behara, D. L. Dotson, R. Galvelis, J. E. Herr, J. T. Horton, Y. Mao, J. D. Chodera, B. P. Pritchard, Y. Wang, G. De Fabritiis, T. E. Markland","year":2023,"title":"SPICE, a dataset of drug-like molecules and peptides for training machine learning potentials","journal":"Scientific Data","vol":"10","pages":"11","doi":"10.1038/s41597-022-01882-6","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"fu2023","authors":"X. Fu, Z. Wu, W. Wang, T. Xie, S. Keten, R. Gomez-Bombarelli, T. Jaakkola","year":2023,"title":"Forces are not enough: Benchmark and critical evaluation for machine learning force fields with molecular simulations","journal":"Transactions on Machine Learning Research","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2210.07237","url":"","note":"","used":["forces","results"]},{"id":"musaelian2023","authors":"A. Musaelian, S. Batzner, A. Johansson, L. Sun, C. J. Owen, M. Kornbluth, B. Kozinsky","year":2023,"title":"Learning local equivariant representations for large-scale atomistic dynamics","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"14","pages":"579","doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-36329-y","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["tensor","arch","practice"]},{"id":"musaelian2023sc","authors":"A. Musaelian, A. Johansson, S. Batzner, B. Kozinsky","year":2023,"title":"Scaling the leading accuracy of deep equivariant models to biomolecular simulations of realistic size","journal":"Proceedings of SC23: International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis","vol":"","pages":"1–12","doi":"10.1145/3581784.3627041","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"The OpenAlex record lists the authors in the wrong order (Kozinsky first); the order here follows the paper itself and the NequIP repository’s citation, with Musaelian first.","used":["practice"]},{"id":"barroso2024","authors":"L. Barroso-Luque, M. Shuaibi, X. Fu, B. M. Wood, M. Dzamba, M. Gao, A. Rizvi, C. L. Zitnick, Z. W. Ulissi","year":2024,"title":"Open Materials 2024 (OMat24) inorganic materials dataset and models","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2410.12771","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"bigi2024","authors":"F. Bigi, M. Langer, M. Ceriotti","year":2024,"title":"The dark side of the forces: assessing non-conservative force models for atomistic machine learning","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2412.11569","url":"","note":"","used":["forces"]},{"id":"batatia2025","authors":"I. Batatia, S. Batzner, D. P. Kovács, A. Musaelian, G. N. C. Simm, R. Drautz, C. Ortner, B. Kozinsky, G. Csányi","year":2025,"title":"The design space of E(3)-equivariant atom-centred interatomic potentials","journal":"Nature Machine Intelligence","vol":"7","pages":"56–67","doi":"10.1038/s42256-024-00956-x","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"kavanagh2026","authors":"S. R. Kavanagh, C. W. Tan, M. Wang, M. L. Descoteaux, G. de Miranda Nascimento, U. Unneberg, L. Zichi, F. Libbi, N. Rivano, A. Glover, V. Bharadwaj, A. Johansson, W. C. Witt, A. Musaelian, B. Kozinsky","year":2026,"title":"Fast and accurate equivariant foundation models for atomistic simulation","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2607.28461","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]},{"id":"tan2026","authors":"C. W. Tan, M. L. Descoteaux, M. Kotak, G. de Miranda Nascimento, S. R. Kavanagh, L. Zichi, M. Wang, A. Saluja, Y. R. Hu, T. Smidt, A. Johansson, W. C. Witt, B. Kozinsky, A. Musaelian","year":2026,"title":"High-performance training and inference for deep equivariant interatomic potentials","journal":"Digital Discovery","vol":"5","pages":"1558–1567","doi":"10.1039/D5DD00423C","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["practice"]}],"chapters":{"why":{"id":"why","body":[{"p":"The rules of molecular dynamics (MD) are simple: know the force on every atom, take a small step by Newton’s equations, compute the forces again, take another step. All the difficulty lies in “compute the forces”. With steps of a few femtoseconds, one nanosecond means a million evaluations. A density functional theory (DFT) force calculation on a few hundred atoms takes minutes to hours on an ordinary computer, so ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) is usually limited to a few hundred atoms and tens of picoseconds."},{"p":"Classical force fields take the other road: write the energy as springs on bond lengths, angles and dihedrals, plus Lennard-Jones and Coulomb terms, with parameters fitted in advance. They are fast enough for millions of atoms and microseconds, but the functional form is chosen by people, and the classic limitation is that the bond term is harmonic: a bond can stretch, but it never breaks and no new one forms. The demo below puts a Morse curve next to its harmonic approximation."},{"demo":"pes"},{"h":"Machine-learned potentials: learning the surface from data"},{"p":"The idea of a machine-learned interatomic potential (MLIP) is: prescribe no functional form, but take a set of structures computed by quantum chemistry (coordinates, energies, forces) as training data, and fit a sufficiently flexible function to the potential-energy surface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Training takes some effort; afterwards the cost per step is proportional to the number of atoms and orders of magnitude below DFT."},{"p":"Almost every method shares a formulation that began with Behler and Parrinello in 2007 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-behler2007\" data-ref=\"behler2007\">Behler &amp; Parrinello 2007</a>]</span>: the total energy is a sum of “atomic energies”, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> depends only on the environment of atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> within a cutoff radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. This assumption brings two benefits: the cost grows linearly with the number of atoms, and a model trained on small systems can be applied to large ones. It is also an approximation: long-range interactions such as electrostatics and dispersion are cut off, which chapter 8 returns to."},{"h":"From symmetry functions to graph neural networks"},{"p":"The first generation turned the local environment into a list of numbers that do not change under rotation, translation or exchange of like atoms, using hand-designed “descriptors”, then attached a shallow neural network (Behler–Parrinello) or a Gaussian process (GAP) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-behler2007\" data-ref=\"behler2007\">Behler &amp; Parrinello 2007</a>; <a href=\"#ref-bartok2010\" data-ref=\"bartok2010\">Bartók et al. 2010</a>]</span>. The ANI family extended this to organic molecules with millions of DFT structures <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-smith2017\" data-ref=\"smith2017\">Smith et al. 2017</a>]</span>; sGDML fits forces directly with kernel methods and is very accurate on small molecules <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a>; <a href=\"#ref-chmiela2018\" data-ref=\"chmiela2018\">Chmiela et al. 2018</a>]</span>; DeePMD also combines descriptors with deep networks, trained on more than a hundred thousand structures of water and ice <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhang2018\" data-ref=\"zhang2018\">Zhang et al. 2018</a>]</span>. The atomic cluster expansion (ACE) provides a systematic, complete set of many-body descriptors <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-drautz2019\" data-ref=\"drautz2019\">Drautz 2019</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The second generation is graph neural networks (GNNs): atoms are nodes, pairs within the cutoff are edges, and the network learns features of the environment itself by passing “messages” along the graph layer by layer <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gilmer2017\" data-ref=\"gilmer2017\">Gilmer et al. 2017</a>]</span>. SchNet is the archetype <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schutt2017\" data-ref=\"schutt2017\">Schütt et al. 2017</a>]</span>; DimeNet adds bond angles to the messages <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a>]</span>. But their features are all scalars (distances and angles are rotation-invariant numbers), and that is exactly what NequIP changes."},{"h":"What NequIP is"},{"p":"NequIP (Neural Equivariant Interatomic Potentials) was introduced by Batzner and colleagues in Boris Kozinsky’s group at Harvard; the preprint appeared on arXiv in January 2021 and the paper in <i>Nature Communications</i> in 2022 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. It is still a graph neural network that sums atomic energies and takes forces as the negative gradient of the energy; the difference is that each atom carries not only scalar features but also vectors and higher-order geometric tensors, and every operation in the network is <b>equivariant</b> under rotation, inversion and translation (chapter 2). The paper’s conclusion: such models reach the best accuracy of the time on small molecules, water, a catalytic surface, a glass and a lithium-ion conductor, and need training sets as small as a few hundred or even a hundred structures, up to three orders of magnitude fewer than other methods."},{"p":"This section goes in this order: first the three mathematical tools, equivariance, spherical harmonics and tensor products (chapters 2 to 4); then NequIP’s network taken apart (chapter 5); why forces should come from differentiating the energy (chapter 6); the experiments and numbers in the paper (chapter 7); and finally training, deployment, its successors and the open problems (chapter 8). For a review, see Unke et al. 2021 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-unke2021\" data-ref=\"unke2021\">Unke et al. 2021</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>The accuracy ceiling is the training data.</b> A machine-learned potential learns the potential-energy surface of its reference method. Train on PBE data and you get a faster PBE, systematic errors of the functional included; to do better you need a more expensive reference. NequIP’s data efficiency makes expensive references such as coupled-cluster CCSD(T) feasible (chapter 7).","kind":"warn"}],"points":[{"h":"MD is bottlenecked by force evaluations","p":"With steps of a few femtoseconds a nanosecond needs a million force evaluations; DFT takes minutes to hours each, so ab initio MD usually covers a few hundred atoms for tens of picoseconds.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Classical force-field bonds never break","p":"A harmonic bond term cannot describe bonds forming or breaking, the price of fixing the functional form in advance.","st":"proved"},{"h":"E = Σ E_i is an assumption","p":"Splitting the energy by atom, each seeing only its environment within the cutoff: linear cost and transfer to large systems, but long-range interactions are cut off.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Three generations","p":"Hand-designed descriptors with shallow models (BPNN, GAP, ANI); graph neural networks with scalar features (SchNet, DimeNet); equivariant graph neural networks (NequIP and after).","st":"history"},{"h":"NequIP’s two claims","p":"State-of-the-art accuracy at the time, and up to three orders of magnitude less training data. Both were verified on the paper’s benchmarks; chapter 7 has the numbers.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The ceiling is the reference method","p":"However good the model, it can only approach the quantum-chemistry method used for its training data.","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Molecular dynamics: the force is the negative gradient of the potential energy."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">∣</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Local decomposition: each atomic energy sees only neighbours within the cutoff r_c <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-behler2007\" data-ref=\"behler2007\">Behler &amp; Parrinello 2007</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two curves of the demo: the Morse potential and its harmonic approximation at the minimum."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mi>N</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"How the cost grows with the number of atoms: linear for local models, roughly cubic for conventional DFT (diagonalisation)."}],"example":{"q":"In the Morse potential (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), stretch the bond to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. How much higher is the harmonic energy than the true one? At what distance does the harmonic potential reach the dissociation energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>?","steps":["Morse: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4066</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4066</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.352</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Harmonic: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.36</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.81</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, an excess of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.46</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, 2.3 times the true value.","Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.667</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.67</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Beyond that the harmonic energy keeps rising while the true curve has nearly levelled off."],"a":"Move the probe to 1.60 in the demo and the harmonic error reads 0.458 D. A machine-learned potential trained on data that include stretched and broken bonds can learn the whole curve; in regions the training data do not cover, it is no more reliable than the harmonic spring."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-behler2007\" data-ref=\"behler2007\">Behler &amp; Parrinello 2007</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bartok2010\" data-ref=\"bartok2010\">Bartók et al. 2010</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gilmer2017\" data-ref=\"gilmer2017\">Gilmer et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-schutt2017\" data-ref=\"schutt2017\">Schütt et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-smith2017\" data-ref=\"smith2017\">Smith et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chmiela2018\" data-ref=\"chmiela2018\">Chmiela et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zhang2018\" data-ref=\"zhang2018\">Zhang et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-drautz2019\" data-ref=\"drautz2019\">Drautz 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-unke2021\" data-ref=\"unke2021\">Unke et al. 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>"},"symmetry":{"id":"symmetry","body":[{"p":"Shift a water molecule, rotate it, reflect it in a mirror: its energy does not change at all. But its dipole moment and the force on each atom are vectors, and they rotate along with the molecule. A good potential must obey these rules exactly; otherwise the same molecule placed differently would give different results, and simulations would show spurious drifts and rotations."},{"h":"Groups, representations and the definition of equivariance"},{"p":"The distance-preserving transformations of three-dimensional space (translations, rotations, inversions and their combinations) form a group, written E(3); rotations and inversions alone form O(3), and rotations alone SO(3). A group element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can act in different ways on different spaces, called <b>representations</b> of the group: on coordinates it is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> rotation matrix, on a scalar it does nothing, on a rank-2 tensor it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"A function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is <b>equivariant</b> under a group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> if transforming then computing gives the same as computing then transforming: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. <b>Invariance</b> is the special case where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the identity: the energy is invariant under E(3). The forces, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, are equivariant, and so are the dipole moment and the polarisability tensor."},{"demo":"equiv"},{"p":"The equivariant model in the demo sits at errors of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: that is floating-point round-off, not an approximation. Equivariance is guaranteed by construction, not learned. The non-equivariant model merely multiplies the same sum by a fixed matrix, and its error is of order one tenth. Many early networks consumed raw coordinates and could only learn rotational symmetry approximately through “data augmentation” (rotating the training set at random many times), which wastes data and is never exact."},{"h":"Are invariants enough?"},{"p":"The easy way is to feed only invariants (interatomic distances, bond angles) so that the output is automatically invariant; SchNet and DimeNet do this. The trouble is that the intermediate features passed from layer to layer are also all scalars, and direction can only be encoded indirectly in “combinations of distances and angles”. For instance, whether two neighbours sit on opposite sides of the central atom or on the same side cannot be told from their distances to the centre alone; to tell them apart one must add angles, dihedrals, ever more many-body terms."},{"p":"Equivariant networks keep direction in the features: each atom carries scalars, vectors and higher-order tensors. For example, summing the neighbours’ unit vectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> weighted by a function of distance gives a vector feature that says “roughly which way the neighbours lie”; the equivariant model of the demo is exactly this step. Vectors combined with vectors give scalars (dot products), new vectors (cross products) and rank-2 tensors, far richer information than distances alone. NequIP builds on the ideas of Tensor Field Networks <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a>]</span> and 3D steerable convolutions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a>]</span> and is implemented with the e3nn library <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>]</span>; earlier group-equivariant convolutional networks are due to Cohen and Welling <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cohen2016\" data-ref=\"cohen2016\">Cohen &amp; Welling 2016</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Equivariance and data efficiency.</b> The paper attributes NequIP’s data efficiency to equivariance: with identical settings, a network restricted to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (scalars only) has a learning-curve slope like other methods; once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is used, the slope changes (chapter 7). Why equivariance changes the slope of the learning curve, the paper itself says, still lacks a clear theoretical explanation.","kind":"info"},{"h":"Translation, permutation and parity"},{"p":"<b>Translation</b> is the easiest: the network uses only relative positions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so shifting everything changes nothing. <b>Permutation</b>: exchanging the labels of like atoms must leave the energy unchanged. A graph network sums over neighbours, and a sum does not depend on order, so each atom’s features are relabelled along with it and the total energy is unchanged <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. <b>Parity</b> is inversion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: ordinary vectors (position, force, dipole) change sign and have odd parity; “pseudovectors” such as angular momentum or the cross product of two vectors do not, and have even parity. NequIP labels every feature with both a rotation order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and a parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so it is equivariant under the whole of E(3), not just under rotations."},{"p":"The two enantiomers of a chiral molecule have the same energy (in the absence of external fields), yet each is the mirror image of the other. A model equivariant under inversion automatically gives them the same energy; one equivariant only under SO(3) and blind to parity could in principle give them different energies, which is physically wrong (ignoring tiny parity violations such as the weak interaction)."}],"points":[{"h":"Definition of equivariance","p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: transforming then computing equals computing then transforming. Invariance is the special case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Energy invariant, forces equivariant","p":"The energy is a scalar under E(3); forces and dipoles are vectors and the polarisability a rank-2 tensor, all rotating with the molecule.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Equivariance is guaranteed by construction","p":"A network built from equivariant operations is exactly equivariant to floating-point precision, without needing or relying on data augmentation.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Invariants alone lose direction","p":"Scalar features can only encode direction indirectly through many-body distances and angles; equivariant networks keep vectors and tensors in the features directly.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Parity","p":"Each feature is labelled (l, p): an ordinary vector is 1o, a pseudovector such as a cross product is 1e. Equivariance under inversion guarantees equal energies for enantiomers.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Why equivariance saves data","p":"The paper’s controlled experiments support “equivariance changes the slope of the learning curve”, but the reason has no theoretical explanation yet.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Definition of equivariance (equation 1 of the paper)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The energy is invariant and the forces equivariant under E(3)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The single “vector message” of the demo’s equivariant model."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">P</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>1o</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>1e</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Inversion: ordinary vectors are odd, cross products even."}],"example":{"q":"A model outputs for each atom the vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> a fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> matrix. Which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> make it equivariant under every rotation?","steps":["Call the sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; it is equivariant: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Equivariance requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for every rotation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","The only matrices commuting with every rotation are multiples of the identity, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>I</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (Schur’s lemma: the three-dimensional rotation representation is irreducible)."],"a":"So for the “multiply by a matrix” step to be equivariant, it can only multiply by a number. That is exactly NequIP’s rule: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> components of a given order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> must share one weight, or equivariance is lost (chapter 5). The demo’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is not a multiple of the identity, hence the non-zero error."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-cohen2016\" data-ref=\"cohen2016\">Cohen &amp; Welling 2016</a> · <a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>"},"irreps":{"id":"irreps","body":[{"p":"The previous chapter said that equivariant networks carry vectors and higher-order tensors in their features. But how should “higher order” be organised? An ordinary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> under rotation, all nine components mixing. It can, however, be split into three parts that rotate independently: the trace (1 number, unchanged), the antisymmetric part (3 numbers, behaving like a vector) and the symmetric traceless part (5 numbers). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and none of the pieces splits further: they are the <b>irreducible representations</b> of the rotation group, of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and dimension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"This is no stranger to chemistry: s, p, d and f orbitals are the irreducible representations with l = 0, 1, 2 and 3, of which there are 1, 3, 5 and 7. For an electron in a spherically symmetric potential, after a rotation a set of p orbitals is still that set of p orbitals, merely recombined among themselves; p and d never mix."},{"h":"Spherical harmonics"},{"p":"The spherical harmonics <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> are functions on the unit sphere, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. In real form the three of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> are proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the five of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, the same as the angular parts of d orbitals. They are mutually orthogonal, and each order mixes only within itself under rotation:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Wigner D matrix D^l(R) is a (2l + 1) × (2l + 1) orthogonal matrix; for l = 1 it is the rotation matrix itself (with the components reordered)."},{"demo":"sh"},{"p":"Rotate a function such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>20</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in the demo and the bar chart redistributes the coefficients among the five <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, yet their sum of squares stays 1 and coefficients of other <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> stay zero. Those two facts are what “orthogonal” and “irreducible” mean. For an atom, putting the directions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of its neighbours into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> gives a set of numbers that rotate with the molecule: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> term is a constant, the three <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> terms are the unit vector itself, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and higher describe finer details of how the neighbours are arranged."},{"h":"Parity and the names of irreps"},{"p":"O(3) adds inversion to SO(3). Under inversion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, so the spherical harmonics themselves have parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. But a feature need not have parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: the cross product of two ordinary vectors has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and even parity. So the irreducible representations of O(3) carry two labels <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, written in e3nn as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, …, where e means even and o odd <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>]</span>."},{"p":"NequIP’s features are direct sums of several such irreducible representations, each with a number of “channels”: the paper writes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the atom, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the channel and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the component within the order <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. For example, “32x0e + 32x0o + 32x1o + 32x1e” is the setting of the paper’s Table VII for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> with 32 features: per atom, 32 even scalars, 32 odd scalars (pseudoscalars), 32 vectors and 32 pseudovectors, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></semantics></math> numbers in all."},{"note":"<b>Why spherical harmonics rather than Cartesian tensors?</b> Cartesian tensors can also build equivariant networks, but a rank-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></semantics></math> Cartesian tensor has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> components containing many lower-order irreducible parts mixed together (just as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> matrix hides a scalar and a vector). With irreducible representations each order has only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> numbers that never mix, and the rules for combining them (the Clebsch–Gordan coefficients of the next chapter) are the simplest. The price is handling less intuitive objects like Wigner D and CG coefficients, which is what libraries such as e3nn are for.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"An irrep of order l has 2l + 1 dimensions","p":"l = 0 scalar, l = 1 vector, l = 2 five-dimensional; the same counts as s, p and d orbitals.","st":"proved"},{"h":"3 × 3 = 1 + 3 + 5","p":"Any rank-2 tensor splits into its trace, antisymmetric part and symmetric traceless part, each rotating on its own.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Wigner D matrices","p":"A rotation acts on order l as a (2l + 1)-dimensional orthogonal matrix with D(R₁R₂) = D(R₁)D(R₂); different l never mix.","st":"proved"},{"h":"O(3) labels are (l, p)","p":"Spherical harmonics have parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, but features can have the other one: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is a pseudovector, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math> a pseudoscalar.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Features are direct sums of irreps","p":"“32x0e + 32x0o + 32x1o + 32x1e” is 256 numbers per atom; the paper varies l_max from 0 to 3 for comparison.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></munder><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Spherical harmonics mix only within their order under rotation; D is a representation of the group."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Orthonormality of the real spherical harmonics (the normalisation used in the demo)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Parity of the spherical harmonics."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"NequIP’s features: atom a, channel c, component m within the order <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⊕</mo><munder><munder><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>antisym.</mtext></munder><mo>⊕</mo><munder><munder><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>sym. traceless</mtext></munder></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"A rank-2 tensor decomposed into irreducible parts."}],"example":{"q":"A NequIP model has 64 channels, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and both parities at every order. How many numbers does each atom’s feature contain? And if only the half with parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is kept?","steps":["Per channel: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> with two parities each, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></semantics></math> numbers.","64 channels: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1152</mn></mrow></semantics></math> numbers.","Keeping only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>576</mn></mrow></semantics></math> numbers."],"a":"The feature size grows as the square of l_max (Σ(2l + 1) = (l_max + 1)²), and the cost grows faster still, because the tensor product has more paths (next chapter). That is why the paper goes up to l = 3 on small molecules while the official tutorial defaults to l_max = 1."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>"},"tensor":{"id":"tensor","body":[{"p":"With scalar, vector and higher-order features in hand, the network also needs a way to <b>combine</b> them, a multiplication that keeps equivariance. Element-wise products will not do: multiply the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math> component of one vector by the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math> component of another and the result changes when the axes are turned. The correct product is the tensor product: multiply all components of the two features pairwise, then recombine them with fixed coefficients into new irreducible representations."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"C are the Clebsch–Gordan coefficients, fixed by the three l and three m and independent of the network’s parameters."},{"p":"Chemistry has met this long ago: two p electrons couple to total angular momentum L = 0, 1 or 2 (S, P and D terms); that is exactly 1 ⊗ 1 = 0 ⊕ 1 ⊕ 2. Quantum mechanics calls it angular-momentum coupling, with the same Clebsch–Gordan coefficients."},{"h":"Two selection rules"},{"p":"The output order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> must satisfy the triangle inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and parities multiply, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. So two ordinary vectors (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) can only give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: the dot product is a true scalar and the cross product a <b>pseudo</b>vector. These two rules fix which “paths” exist in the network, and therefore how many groups of learnable weights each layer has. The upper half of the demo lists them all."},{"demo":"tp"},{"h":"NequIP’s convolution"},{"p":"The convolution of tensor field networks <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a>]</span> and NequIP <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span> multiplies three things together: the feature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of neighbour <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the spherical harmonics <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> of the direction from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and a radial function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> that depends only on the distance:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Equation (4) of the paper: every allowed triple of filter l_f, input l_i and output l_o is a path with its own radial function. The sum runs only over neighbours inside the cutoff."},{"p":"The radial function sees only the scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, while the spherical harmonics and the input feature rotate with their own D matrices, and the CG coefficients assemble them into an output that rotates with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: the convolution is therefore strictly equivariant, and its learnable part (the radial function) never touches direction. This is the core idea of equivariant network design: <b>all directional information goes to fixed, exact mathematical objects, and the network learns only direction-free scalars</b>."},{"note":"<b>Cost.</b> The number of paths grows quickly with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and each path sums over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> components, so the tensor product is the most expensive operation in such networks. Later work cuts the cost in several ways: MACE puts many-body interactions into a symmetric product within one layer and uses only two layers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a>]</span>; Allegro becomes strictly local so it can be split up and parallelised <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>]</span>; and newer versions of NequIP itself rewrite the tensor-product kernels (chapter 8).","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"l₁ ⊗ l₂ = |l₁ − l₂| ⊕ … ⊕ (l₁ + l₂)","p":"The dimensions add up: (2l₁ + 1)(2l₂ + 1) = Σ(2l_o + 1); for example 3 × 3 = 1 + 3 + 5.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Parities multiply","p":"1o ⊗ 1o gives only even parity: the dot product is a scalar, the cross product a pseudovector.","st":"proved"},{"h":"CG coefficients are fixed","p":"They are fixed by group theory and not trained; the same coefficients serve angular-momentum coupling in atomic physics.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Convolution = feature ⊗ spherical harmonics × radial weight","p":"The learnable radial function depends only on distance, so equivariance comes from construction, not training.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The path count grows with l_max","p":"The tensor product is the most expensive step in equivariant networks; MACE, Allegro and new kernels attack its cost from different directions.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two selection rules of the tensor product."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Conservation of dimension."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">y</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Two vectors: l = 0 is the dot product and l = 1 the cross product (the constants depend on the normalisation; in the demo they are −1/√3 and 1/√2)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Equivariance of the tensor product, the defining property of the CG coefficients."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"NequIP’s equivariant convolution <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"Features and filters both go up to order 1, and only features of parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) are kept. Which paths can an input feature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and the filter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) take?","steps":["Triangle inequality: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>; the maximum order is 1, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is dropped.","Parity: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is not among the kept features, so only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> remains: the dot-product path."],"a":"Keeping only “natural” parity discards pseudovector paths like the cross product. The NequIP paper keeps both parities by default (the official configuration has a parity switch), and the demo’s path table can switch between the two settings to compare the counts."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>"},"arch":{"id":"arch","body":[{"p":"The previous three chapters prepared the parts. This chapter assembles them following the paper’s description <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. The overall idea is the same as in other “sum of atomic energies” models: the total energy is a sum of atomic energies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, each depending only on the surroundings of atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. This lets the model handle systems of any size and makes the cost grow linearly with the number of atoms."},{"h":"Layer by layer"},{"p":"<b>Embedding.</b> Each atom’s species is encoded as a one-hot vector and turned by a linear “self-interaction” into a number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> scalar channels. The first layer’s input therefore contains only scalars: directional information enters at the first convolution, through the spherical harmonics of the neighbour directions."},{"p":"<b>Interaction blocks.</b> Each block applies a self-interaction (linear mixing among channels of the same atom and the same irrep, never mixing different <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math>), the equivariant convolution of the previous chapter, another self-interaction, and then a nonlinearity. An equivariant nonlinearity cannot act on vector components directly; NequIP uses <b>gates</b>: even scalars go through SiLU and odd scalars through tanh, and every feature with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is multiplied by a “gate” computed from scalars <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a>]</span>. Finally the result is added to the block’s input (as in a residual network). The paper uses 5 blocks for molecules and 6 for periodic systems."},{"p":"<b>Output.</b> After the last block only the scalar channels are kept, reduced by two self-interactions to 16 and then to 1: that is the atomic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Each element also has a learnable scale and shift (initialised to 1 and 0), placing the atomic energies of different elements in a suitable range."},{"h":"The radial function"},{"p":"The radial function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> in the convolution first expands the distance in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Bessel functions, multiplies by a polynomial envelope, and feeds the result to a multilayer perceptron with 3 hidden layers of 64 neurons and SiLU activations <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. The envelope comes from DimeNet <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a>]</span>: it makes the function and its first two derivatives vanish at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so energy and forces stay continuous as atoms cross the cutoff sphere. The paper also divides the convolution sum by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>N</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>N</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow></semantics></math> is the average number of neighbours per atom in the training set, so that feature magnitudes do not change with density."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Equation (6) of the paper and the DimeNet envelope, x = r/r_c, p = 6. The nπ are also learned during training."},{"demo":"radial"},{"h":"The receptive field"},{"p":"Each interaction block moves information one step along the graph, so after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> blocks the energy of atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> depends on atoms within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> steps on the graph, reaching at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in space. That is much larger than the cutoff: with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å and 6 blocks the model can nominally “see” 30 Å away. This helps accuracy (more many-body information) but hurts parallelism: when a large system is split across many GPUs, the “ghost atom” region each part must exchange thickens with every layer. Allegro therefore becomes strictly local, using just one sphere of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"graph"},{"h":"Compared with contemporaries"},{"p":"SchNet’s continuous-filter convolution uses only scalars and distances <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schutt2017\" data-ref=\"schutt2017\">Schütt et al. 2017</a>]</span>, DimeNet adds bond angles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a>]</span>, and the features of these models are all invariant. PaiNN has vector features, but only up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schutt2021\" data-ref=\"schutt2021\">Schütt et al. 2021</a>]</span>; Cormorant uses spherical harmonics and CG products of any order, but its paper did not do forces or periodic systems <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anderson2019\" data-ref=\"anderson2019\">Anderson et al. 2019</a>]</span>; GNNFF predicts forces directly, without an energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-park2021\" data-ref=\"park2021\">Park et al. 2021</a>]</span>. NequIP’s combination is: any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, both parities, forces as the derivative of the energy, and periodic systems."}],"points":[{"h":"Sum of atomic energies","p":"E = Σ E_i with each E_i seeing only the environment within the cutoff; cost grows linearly with the number of atoms.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Gated nonlinearity","p":"Scalars go through SiLU or tanh directly; features with l &gt; 0 are multiplied by a gate computed from scalars, preserving equivariance.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Bessel basis + envelope","p":"8 Bessel functions, a p = 6 polynomial envelope and a 3 × 64 perceptron; value and first two derivatives vanish at r_c.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Receptive field T × r_c","p":"Message passing widens the view but makes multi-GPU runs exchange thicker boundaries; Allegro answers with strict locality.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The paper’s settings","p":"Molecules: 5 blocks, r_c = 4 Å (MD-17); periodic systems: 6 blocks, r_c = 4.5–6 Å; one scale and shift per element.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The total energy is a sum of atomic energies; one scale σ and shift μ per element."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Bessel radial basis (equation 6 of the paper)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The envelope goes smoothly to zero at the cutoff <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The gated nonlinearity: the gate g is an extra computed scalar."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>nb</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The average number of neighbours grows as r_c³, and so does the cost of the convolution."}],"example":{"q":"The atomic number density of liquid water is about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.100</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å (the paper’s setting for water and ice), how many neighbours does an atom have on average? And with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>0.100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>90.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; excluding the atom itself, about 90 neighbours.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>0.100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>26.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, about 26 neighbours.","Ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: the number of edges and the convolution cost differ by the same factor."],"a":"The cutoff radius is the most direct knob between accuracy and speed. The “before you start” demo in chapter 8 lets you adjust it together with the number of layers and l_max."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-schutt2017\" data-ref=\"schutt2017\">Schütt et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-anderson2019\" data-ref=\"anderson2019\">Anderson et al. 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-klicpera2020\" data-ref=\"klicpera2020\">Klicpera et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-park2021\" data-ref=\"park2021\">Park et al. 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-schutt2021\" data-ref=\"schutt2021\">Schütt et al. 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>"},"forces":{"id":"forces","body":[{"p":"NequIP outputs a single number: the total energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Forces are obtained by differentiating with respect to the atomic coordinates, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Neural-network frameworks (PyTorch) can differentiate the whole network automatically, as exactly as an analytic derivative, at roughly the cost of one more pass through the network <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>."},{"p":"This has three advantages. First, the forces are automatically equivariant: the energy is invariant, so its gradient rotates with the molecule (chapter 2). Second, the forces are automatically <b>conservative</b>: being the gradient of an energy, they have zero curl, and the work done around any closed path is zero. Third, energy and forces are consistent: the same model can run molecular dynamics, optimise geometries, search for transition states and compute vibrational frequencies (differentiating the forces once more gives the Hessian). GDML had already stressed the importance of an “energy-conserving force field” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a>]</span>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo>∮</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Forces are the negative gradient of the energy and therefore conservative; stress is the derivative of the energy with respect to strain (sign conventions differ between programs)."},{"demo":"nve"},{"p":"In the demo, a tiny amount of curl in the forces makes the total energy of an NVE simulation drift steadily. Real “direct-force” models are not so tidy, but the same problem exists: Fu and co-workers’ benchmark found that low force errors do not guarantee stable simulations, and some models with very low force errors diverge or give wrong structures in long runs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fu2023\" data-ref=\"fu2023\">Fu et al. 2023</a>]</span>; Bigi and co-workers studied non-conservative forces specifically and concluded that they can be used for speed, but are best combined with conservative forces (for instance in multiple-time-step integration), otherwise energy drift and runaway temperatures are hard to avoid <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bigi2024\" data-ref=\"bigi2024\">Bigi et al. 2024</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Force labels and the loss function"},{"p":"From the training side, forces are valuable data too. One DFT calculation gives 1 energy and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> force components, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></semantics></math> structures provide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> labels in all <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. Fitting the forces constrains the shape of the energy surface directly, which matters for data efficiency <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a>]</span>. NequIP’s loss function adds the two with weights:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∥</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>α</mi></munder><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∥</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Equation (7) of the paper. The default is λ_E : λ_F = 1 : N² (N the number of atoms); later versions make every term’s weight configurable."},{"p":"Note that training must differentiate the “force error” once more (with respect to the network parameters), which is a second derivative. Training is therefore somewhat more expensive than fitting energies alone, but each structure contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> more labels in return."},{"note":"<b>Stress and periodic systems.</b> Isobaric (NPT) simulations of crystals and liquids need the stress. It is done like the forces: multiply the cell and all coordinates by a symmetric strain matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, differentiate the energy with respect to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and divide by the volume. NequIP’s periodic version gives the stress by automatic differentiation as well, and DFT stresses can also be put into the training loss.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Predict only the energy","p":"Forces = −∇E come exactly from automatic differentiation at roughly the cost of one more network pass.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Conservative ⇒ no NVE energy drift","p":"A gradient field has zero curl and zero work around closed paths; any remaining drift comes only from the time step.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Low force error ≠ stable simulations","p":"Benchmarks find that models with very low force errors can still diverge in long simulations.","st":"tested"},{"h":"3N + 1 labels per structure","p":"Force data constrain the shape of the energy surface directly, one reason why few structures suffice.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Non-conservative forces can speed things up","p":"Combining them with conservative forces (for instance with multiple time steps) is an active line of work.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Forces from the energy."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟺</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>∮</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Two statements of a conservative force."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The paper’s loss function and default weights <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Stress is the derivative of the energy with respect to strain."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"One more derivative gives the Hessian, from which vibrational frequencies follow."}],"example":{"q":"The structures of the LiPS training set each have 83 atoms. How many labels does the paper’s largest training set of 2500 structures provide? What is the default force weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>)?","steps":["Per structure: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>83</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></semantics></math> labels.","2500 structures: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2500</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>250</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>625</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>000</mn></mrow></semantics></math> labels, of which only 2500 are energies.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>6889</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The force term in the loss is already divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and this large weight puts the per-atom force error on a footing comparable with the total-energy error."],"a":"Force labels outnumber energies 249 to 1. The “what force labels are worth” demo in chapter 7 isolates this with a one-dimensional Gaussian process: with the same four points, adding derivatives clearly lowers the fitting error."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fu2023\" data-ref=\"fu2023\">Fu et al. 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bigi2024\" data-ref=\"bigi2024\">Bigi et al. 2024</a>"},"results":{"id":"results","body":[{"p":"The most striking conclusion of the NequIP paper is not that one particular number is the lowest, but <b>data efficiency</b>: reaching the same or better accuracy with far fewer training structures <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. This chapter lays out the numbers from the paper’s tables as they are, and the demo lets readers look for themselves. All numbers come from the tables of version 3 of the paper on arXiv."},{"h":"Small molecules: the effect of l"},{"p":"MD-17 consists of ab initio molecular dynamics trajectories of ten small molecules at 500 K <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a>]</span>; revised MD-17 recomputes 100 000 of its configurations with tighter convergence (PBE/def2-SVP), removing the numerical noise of the original <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a>]</span>. The paper uses 1000 configurations per molecule (950 training, 50 validation) and compares <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> with methods such as FCHL19 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a>]</span>, GAP <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bartok2010\" data-ref=\"bartok2010\">Bartók et al. 2010</a>]</span>, ANI <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-smith2017\" data-ref=\"smith2017\">Smith et al. 2017</a>]</span> and ACE <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kovacs2021\" data-ref=\"kovacs2021\">Kovács et al. 2021</a>]</span>. Aspirin’s force error falls from 41.9 meV/Å at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> to 8.5 meV/Å at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"The biggest step is from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; beyond that the gains shrink. NequIP with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> has only scalar features, in the same category as invariant networks like SchNet, and its errors are among the worst in the table. The paper also compares with sGDML <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chmiela2018\" data-ref=\"chmiela2018\">Chmiela et al. 2018</a>]</span> on small-molecule data at CCSD(T) accuracy: the force error for aspirin is 8.3 against 33.0 meV/Å."},{"demo":"curve"},{"h":"Water and ice: a 1000-fold data gap"},{"p":"Zhang and co-workers’ DeepMD trained liquid water and three ice phases on 133 500 structures <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhang2018\" data-ref=\"zhang2018\">Zhang et al. 2018</a>]</span>. NequIP uses only 133 of them (a thousandth, trained on forces only) and reaches a force RMSE of 12.5 meV/Å for liquid water against DeepMD’s 40.4. A second water dataset comes from Cheng and co-workers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cheng2019\" data-ref=\"cheng2019\">Cheng et al. 2019</a>]</span>: their Behler–Parrinello network reaches an RMSE of about 120 meV/Å with 1303 structures, while NequIP gives 129.8 with 100 structures and 103.4 with 250."},{"h":"Learning curves"},{"p":"In machine learning, test error and training-set size often follow a power law <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, a straight line on log–log axes whose slope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math> depends on the model and the problem <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hestness2017\" data-ref=\"hestness2017\">Hestness et al. 2017</a>]</span>. On LiPS (a lithium-ion conductor of 83 atoms) the paper trains on 10, 100, 1000 and 2500 structures with force errors of 109.9, 28.4, 8.3 and 4.9 meV/Å; the demo fits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></semantics></math> on the spot. The paper’s key observation is that, with the same network and only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> changed, the slope at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> resembles other methods while the slope for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is steeper; an appendix controls for the number of weights and features, ruling out “just more parameters”."},{"note":"<b>This is not yet explained.</b> The paper states plainly that why equivariance changes the slope of the learning curve, and how much the many-body information from message passing contributes, are both unclear. One intuition: an invariant network has to “learn” how things behave under rotation from data, whereas an equivariant network has it built in, so each training structure counts for every rotation; but that only explains a smaller constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, not a change of slope.","kind":"info"},{"demo":"gpfit"},{"h":"More than errors"},{"p":"Low errors must also run the right physics. The paper runs molecular dynamics with a model trained on 2500 LiPS structures; the Li diffusion coefficient averaged over ten 50 ps trajectories is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm²/s against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.37</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> from AIMD (9% apart). For Li₄P₂O₇, a model trained only on structures of the 3000 K melt describes the radial and angular distribution functions of the amorphous structure quenched to 600 K; and formate on copper is trained on 2500 structures. As chapter 6 noted, “low error” and “correct simulation” are two different things <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fu2023\" data-ref=\"fu2023\">Fu et al. 2023</a>]</span>, so checks like these say more than the numbers in the tables."}],"points":[{"h":"l = 0 → 1 is the biggest step","p":"On revised MD-17 force errors drop to between 1/2 and 1/25 (ethanol to benzene); l = 2 and 3 improve further with shrinking gains.","st":"tested"},{"h":"133 against 133 500","p":"Water and ice: with a thousandth of the structures, NequIP’s force RMSE is a quarter to a half of DeepMD’s.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The learning-curve slope changes","p":"Equivariant models have steeper log–log learning curves, even with the number of parameters controlled.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Why the slope changes","p":"The paper itself says there is no clear theoretical explanation yet.","st":"open"},{"h":"Checks on physical quantities","p":"LiPS Li diffusivity within 9% of AIMD; Li₄P₂O₇ extrapolates from the melt to the amorphous solid.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>a</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The power law of learning curves <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hestness2017\" data-ref=\"hestness2017\">Hestness et al. 2017</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>LiPS</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Fitted to the four points of Table VI of the paper (computed live in the demo)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mi>lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The diffusion coefficient from the mean-square displacement (the Einstein relation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>∑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mo>≥</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two error measures in the tables; check which one is being compared."}],"example":{"q":"With the LiPS power law <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, by what factor must the training set grow to halve the force error? Check against the paper’s points: going from 1000 to 2500 structures, what should the error fall to?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> times.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1000</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.56</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.60</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV/Å.","The paper reports 4.9 meV/Å, in line with the power law."],"a":"The steeper the slope, the more each extra datum is worth. But a fit to four points describes only this dataset; a different system, error measure (MAE or RMSE) or training setup changes the slope."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bartok2010\" data-ref=\"bartok2010\">Bartók et al. 2010</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chmiela2017\" data-ref=\"chmiela2017\">Chmiela et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hestness2017\" data-ref=\"hestness2017\">Hestness et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-smith2017\" data-ref=\"smith2017\">Smith et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chmiela2018\" data-ref=\"chmiela2018\">Chmiela et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zhang2018\" data-ref=\"zhang2018\">Zhang et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-cheng2019\" data-ref=\"cheng2019\">Cheng et al. 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-christensen2020\" data-ref=\"christensen2020\">Christensen &amp; von Lilienfeld 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kovacs2021\" data-ref=\"kovacs2021\">Kovács et al. 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fu2023\" data-ref=\"fu2023\">Fu et al. 2023</a>"},"practice":{"id":"practice","body":[{"h":"The workflow of one training run"},{"p":"At the time of the paper NequIP was version 0.3, running on e3nn 0.3.5 and PyTorch 1.9 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>. Version 0.7.0 in April 2025 was a major rewrite, and later versions all follow the same workflow (written here from the official documentation; the documentation is authoritative for commands and options):"},{"p":"<b>Prepare data.</b> A set of DFT structures with energies and forces (plus stresses for periodic systems), usually in a format ASE can read, such as extended XYZ <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-larsen2017\" data-ref=\"larsen2017\">Larsen et al. 2017</a>]</span>. The data must cover the configurations the simulation will meet: high temperatures, stretched bonds, defects, surfaces. Active learning lets the model choose the next structures to compute: FLARE uses the uncertainty of a Gaussian process to decide during a simulation when to call DFT <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-vandermause2020\" data-ref=\"vandermause2020\">Vandermause et al. 2020</a>]</span>."},{"p":"<b>Train.</b> <code>nequip-train</code> reads a YAML configuration (managed with Hydra): dataset and splits, cutoff radius, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, number of layers, number of features, loss weights, optimiser and learning rate. Training produces a checkpoint file, and inference uses the exponential moving average (EMA) of the weights by default."},{"p":"<b>Package and compile.</b> <code>nequip-package</code> packs a checkpoint into a file that can be distributed and read in another environment; <code>nequip-compile</code> compiles a model for a particular program: <code>--target ase</code> for an ASE calculator, or for LAMMPS through its pair style and ML-IAP interface <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-thompson2022\" data-ref=\"thompson2022\">Thompson et al. 2022</a>]</span>. At compile time <code>--modifiers</code> can switch in accelerated GPU kernels such as OpenEquivariance (support for cuEquivariance is still experimental)."},{"demo":"cost"},{"h":"Allegro: strict locality"},{"p":"As chapter 5 explained, the receptive field of message passing grows with the number of layers, which makes parallelism hard. Allegro uses a different structure: features live on <b>pairs of atoms</b> rather than on atoms, and every layer performs tensor products only within the neighbour sphere of the central atom, so information never travels beyond <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a>]</span>. An atom’s energy depends only on atoms within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so a large system can be cut spatially into pieces for many GPUs, each exchanging only a boundary one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> thick. Allegro has simulated systems of hundreds of millions of atoms this way, with scaling over thousands of GPUs reported at SC23 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023sc\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023sc\">Musaelian et al. 2023b</a>]</span>."},{"h":"MACE and the design space"},{"p":"MACE brings the idea of the atomic cluster expansion (ACE) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-drautz2019\" data-ref=\"drautz2019\">Drautz 2019</a>]</span> into equivariant message passing: each layer first aggregates neighbour information and then takes a high-order symmetric product, so a single layer already has four- and five-body interactions and two layers suffice <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a>]</span>. Afterwards the authors of NequIP, Allegro and MACE wrote a joint paper placing such “atom-centred equivariant potentials” in a common design space: many-body order, number of message-passing layers, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, local or semi-local, each with its own costs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batatia2025\" data-ref=\"batatia2025\">Batatia et al. 2025</a>]</span>. No single design is best for every system."},{"h":"Foundation models"},{"p":"The change of the last two years is <b>pretrained general-purpose models</b>: one model trained on large datasets that cover most of the periodic table, such as OMat24 for inorganic materials <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-barroso2024\" data-ref=\"barroso2024\">Barroso-Luque et al. 2024</a>]</span> and SPICE for drug-like molecules and peptides <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eastman2023\" data-ref=\"eastman2023\">Eastman et al. 2023</a>]</span>, used directly or fine-tuned on a small amount of one’s own data. Foundation models with the NequIP and Allegro architectures are published at nequip.net and referred to by IDs such as <code>nequip.net:mir-group/NequIP-OAM-L:0.1</code> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kavanagh2026\" data-ref=\"kavanagh2026\">Kavanagh et al. 2026</a>]</span>; new training and inference acceleration is what made training at that scale feasible <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tan2026\" data-ref=\"tan2026\">Tan et al. 2026</a>]</span>. This site’s <a href=\"../open-models/\">Open Model Hall</a> collects comparable models from other companies (Meta’s UMA among them), and <a href=\"../models/\">the model interface</a> can run some of them online."},{"note":"<b>What it does not know.</b> Long-range electrostatics and dispersion beyond the cutoff, charge transfer, excited states and changes of spin state are not described by standard NequIP; it also inherits the errors of the DFT functional in its training data. Foundation models cover a lot of ground, but for one specific system they are often less accurate than a model trained or fine-tuned on that system’s data. Before use, compare energies, forces and the properties you care about against DFT on at least a few structures. A concrete case: relaxing adsorption structures on bimetallic alloy surfaces directly with UMA’s OC20 task puts only about 68% of adsorption energies within 0.1 eV of DFT, and guesses far from equilibrium do worse (<a href=\"../news/#n-dbcata-adsorption\">Chemistry &amp; materials news · DBCata</a>).","kind":"warn"}],"points":[{"h":"train → package → compile","p":"nequip-train trains from a YAML configuration, nequip-package packages, nequip-compile compiles for ASE or LAMMPS.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Allegro is strictly local","p":"Features on atom pairs never leave r_c, so systems can be split spatially; it has simulated hundreds of millions of atoms.","st":"tested"},{"h":"MACE: high body order, few layers","p":"The symmetric product gives many-body interactions within one layer, so two layers suffice; every choice in the design space has its costs.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Pretrained foundation models","p":"Trained on large datasets covering most of the periodic table, used directly or fine-tuned; accuracy for a specific system must be checked.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"Long-range and electronic effects","p":"Electrostatics beyond the cutoff, charge transfer, excited states and spin still lack a general solution.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Strict locality (Allegro): an atomic energy depends only on atoms within r_c."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>edges</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>atoms</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The number of edges grows linearly with atoms and as r_c³."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>body order</mtext><mo>≤</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> per layer (MACE)</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>receptive field</mtext><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"MACE’s symmetric-product order ν and the receptive field of message passing."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>fastest</mtext></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>O–H stretch: </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>9</mn><mtext> fs</mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext>–</mtext><mn>1</mn><mtext> fs</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"A rule of thumb for the time step: about a tenth of the fastest vibrational period."}],"example":{"q":"A NequIP model with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å and 6 layers simulates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> atoms of water split over 64 GPUs, each holding a cube. How thick a “ghost atom” boundary must each piece fetch from its neighbours? And with Allegro?","steps":["Each piece has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>64</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>625</mn></mrow></semantics></math> atoms, a volume of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>625</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>156</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>250</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å³, and a side of about 54 Å.","NequIP: the boundary is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å thick, so the side becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>54</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>114</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å, and the atoms to process are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>114</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>54</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>9.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> times its own.","Allegro: the boundary is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å thick, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>64</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>54</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> times."],"a":"This is only a rough geometric estimate (real implementations can exchange just the needed features at each layer rather than recompute the whole boundary), but it shows why large-scale parallelism favours strictly local models. Adjust the layers and r_c in the demo and the receptive-field readout follows."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-larsen2017\" data-ref=\"larsen2017\">Larsen et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-drautz2019\" data-ref=\"drautz2019\">Drautz 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-vandermause2020\" data-ref=\"vandermause2020\">Vandermause et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batatia2022\" data-ref=\"batatia2022\">Batatia et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-thompson2022\" data-ref=\"thompson2022\">Thompson et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-eastman2023\" data-ref=\"eastman2023\">Eastman et al. 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023\">Musaelian et al. 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-musaelian2023sc\" data-ref=\"musaelian2023sc\">Musaelian et al. 2023b</a> · <a href=\"#ref-barroso2024\" data-ref=\"barroso2024\">Barroso-Luque et al. 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batatia2025\" data-ref=\"batatia2025\">Batatia et al. 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kavanagh2026\" data-ref=\"kavanagh2026\">Kavanagh et al. 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tan2026\" data-ref=\"tan2026\">Tan et al. 2026</a>"}}};
